course: dictionary
lesson: 4687
title: A word for everyone at home
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A homemaker is a person whose main work is looking after their home and family. They cook, clean and care for the children, instead of having a paid job. Homemaker is a noun, so you can have one homemaker or many homemakers.
explain: Look at the word itself for a clue: home and maker. A homemaker makes a home. When you meet a new word, break it into smaller parts and see whether they give you a hint.
explain: «"Domestic engineer" is a joking name for a housewife, househusband or homemaker.» The joke suggests that running a home takes skill and effort, a bit like an engineer's work. Housewife and househusband describe the same kind of work, but for one gender each.
explain: The phrase «a full-time homemaker» means someone who does this work all day, every day. Full-time is the clue: it means all of the working hours, not just part of them.
fact: A homemaker is a person who looks after a home and family, usually without a paid job.
fact: Homemaker is a noun: a homemaker, homemakers.
fact: «Homemaker» is used for women and men alike.
fact: «Housewife» and «househusband» name the same kind of person, but «homemaker» is a modern, kinder word that fits either one.
fact: Work done at home is often unpaid, but it is important work.
exercise: «She is a full-time homemaker.» What does full-time homemaker mean? | A person who works all day in an office | *A person whose main work is looking after the home and family | A person who sells houses | A person who builds houses
exercise: Which word fits both a woman and a man who looks after the home and family? | Housewife | Househusband | *Homemaker | Cook
exercise: The joking name «domestic engineer» suggests what about running a home? | Only machines can do it | *It takes skill and effort, a bit like an engineer's work | Engineers never work at home | Homemakers are never busy
test: Which is the correct plural of homemaker? | homemakes | *homemakers | homemakeres | homemaker's
test: «Her cousin has a job, but she is a full-time homemaker.» Which phrase means the same as full-time homemaker? | Someone who makes cakes at home | *Someone who looks after the home and family all day | Someone who works part of the day | Someone who runs a shop
exam: Jo says a homemaker must be a woman. Is Jo right? | *No, the word is used for men and women alike | Yes, only women can be homemakers | Yes, but only if she cooks | No, homemakers must have a paid job
